===== 6. the flags, when the table goes stale =====
### Scenario 2: `zs_temp` is a driver this port does not have (so the unit is a guess, and saying so
### beats hiding it), and `emmc_therm` is reporting the milli value a driver that switched units would
### produce -- 40000 for 40 C. The second one is caught by the plausibility band, is printed with its
### raw value, and is then refused a place in the hottest pick.
== thermal zones:
   thermal_zone1 tsens_tz_sensor1             55.8 C
   thermal_zone21 pm8994_tz                    49.1 C
   thermal_zone22 battery                      42.5 C
   thermal_zone23 msm_therm                    49.0 C
   thermal_zone24 quiet_therm                  50.0 C
   thermal_zone25 zs_temp                      41.5 C   <- type not in the unit table; assumed milli-degC (raw 41500)
   thermal_zone26 emmc_therm                40000.0 C   <- IMPLAUSIBLE as degC: raw 40000
   thermal_zone8 tsens_tz_sensor8             58.0 C
   hottest: tsens_tz_sensor8 58.0 C  (raw 580 = deci-degC, of 8 zones; 2 flagged above; 1 implausible, left out of the pick)

### The band is not a substitute for the table and the script says so: a family mis-assumed by 1000x is
### caught (47000 milli read as degC -> 40000 C), a family mis-assumed by 10x is not (580 deci read as
### milli -> 0.58 C, which is a believable temperature). That is why the hottest line prints the raw
### value and the unit as well -- `raw 580 = deci-degC` can be checked in the head.

===== 7. the health check, which is the first number a person reads =====
### It used to read thermal_zone1 -- a tsens sensor, chosen only because "1" looked like a sensible
### index -- and divide by 1000, so it announced "thermal_zone1: 0.6 C" on a phone whose SoC was 55.8 C.
### Now it asks for every zone as type:temp over the one ssh round trip and scales host-side, using the
### same table.
   thermal: hottest of 6 zones: tsens_tz_sensor8 58.0 C (raw 580 = deci-degC)

### the whole device-side command it now sends, verbatim from the script:
197:  echo "thermal=$(for z in /sys/class/thermal/thermal_zone*; do [ -r "$z/temp" ] || continue; printf "%s:%s " "$(cat "$z/type" 2>/dev/null)" "$(cat "$z/temp" 2>/dev/null)"; done)"

### And the anti-drift check: the instrument (device-side loop) and the health check (host-side awk)
### must agree on the same six zones, so the two copies of the table cannot separate silently.
instrument:      hottest: tsens_tz_sensor8 58.0 C  (raw 580 = deci-degC, of 6 zones)
health check:    thermal: hottest of 6 zones: tsens_tz_sensor8 58.0 C (raw 580 = deci-degC)

### The assertion is scoped to the heat line and not to the whole output on purpose: the router's own
### prose explains the bug it used to have ("reported the SoC at 0.6 C"), and an assertion over the
### whole output trips on that explanation rather than on the bug.

===== 8. the self-test, whole =====
### 44 checks, all passing. Sections 1/2 are the unit table, 3 is the A/B, 4 is the read_stat assertion
### doc 81's own bug left behind, 5 the flags, 6 read-only-ness, 7 the health check + the agreement.
